# Architecture

## Layout

```text
crates/core/                       Shared types. Crate name cutlass-core.
crates/codecs/software/<codec>/    Rust codecs. Crate name cutlass-<codec>.
crates/codecs/native/<os-api>/     One crate per OS codec API. Crate name cutlass-<os-api>.
crates/testing/fixtures/           Loads the shared fixtures for codec tests.
fixtures/                          Small generated media. See fixtures/README.md.
docs/                              Design docs. docs/codecs.md is the codec plan. docs/project.md is the project model.
```

- The workspace picks up every crate under `crates/codecs/` and
  `crates/testing/` automatically. `crates/core` is a member on its own.
- A new area gets its own folder under `crates/`, named for what it does, for
  example `crates/containers/mp4`.
- App shells (desktop, iOS, Android) go in `apps/` when they exist. Don't use
  "platform" in the name of media code.

## Boundaries

- Software crates never call an OS SDK or link C code. CI builds them for
  `wasm32-unknown-unknown` to prove it.
- A native crate wraps one OS API and nothing else.
- The choice between native and software happens in one place, above both. A
  codec crate never falls back on its own. It returns "unsupported".
- Codec crates do no I/O. They take compressed packets and the container's codec
  config, and return frames. Encoders do the reverse. Files, URLs, and Android
  content URIs are the caller's job.
- Containers (MP4, MOV, MKV, WebM, AVI) are parsed outside the codec crates.
- Types that cross crates (frames, timestamps, pixel formats) live in one shared
  core crate. Codec crates don't each define their own.
- Codec crates don't depend on other codec crates. Shared codec tools, such as a
  bit reader or a DCT, get their own crate.
- No global mutable state and no singletons. Capabilities and config are values
  passed in.

## Dependency direction

Dependencies point one way, from low to high:

1. Shared core types.
2. Codec crates, both software and native.
3. The media layer that picks a codec.
4. The engine.
5. The UI and app shells.

A crate depends only on levels below it.

## Mobile

- Every crate except the desktop UI builds for `aarch64-apple-ios` and
  `aarch64-linux-android`. CI checks both.
- Don't assume a desktop. No subprocesses, no fixed paths, and no blocking the
  main thread.
